{"text": "This section includes only information that changes how support should be provided. For Noah, the pertinent information is: medical: seizure action plan and medication protocol; physical: positioning and safe transfers; sensory: fluorescent-light fatigue; and behaviour: no primary concern. These details belong in the profile because they affect access, safety, regulation, communication, or independence. They should be shared only with staff who need the information and handled respectfully.\n\nThe priority challenges are the barriers we have already described: motor fatigue and pain; rapid-fire questioning; seizure recovery and missed instruction; and inaccessible field trips or classroom layouts. The EA’s role is not to diagnose, redesign the student’s program independently, or provide constant help. The EA works under teacher direction and follows the IEP, health and safety plans, behaviour-support plans, and specialist recommendations.\n\nFor Noah, the most useful EA actions are to keep AAC mounted, charged, and accessible; follow seizure and transfer plans; protect response time; and plan accessibility before activities begin. In practice, this means preparing access before the activity, watching for the student’s communication, using the least intrusive prompt that works, and fading support when the student can take over.\n\nAcross every activity, four priorities remain constant: dignity, safety, access, and growing independence. We can now use the student’s actual learning goals to decide whether the program is adapted or modified.", "voice": "en-GB-SoniaNeural", "audio": "special-education-D-05.mp3", "words": [{"text": "This", "start": 0.1375, "end": 0.3875, "start_char": 0, "end_char": 4}, {"text": "section", "start": 0.4, "end": 0.8875, "start_char": 5, "end_char": 12}, {"text": "includes", "start": 0.9, "end": 1.475, "start_char": 13, "end_char": 21}, {"text": "only", "start": 1.4875, "end": 1.7625, "start_char": 22, "end_char": 26}, {"text": "in